# 1 ---------------------------------------------- binaural staged rate ramp (k40nANMRnG4)
S("k40nANMRnG4","BINAURAL BEATS IN ABLETON - How to create and how to use them for healing and meditation music",
 "binaural-staged-rate-ramp-meet-listener-in-beta-clip-envelope-presets-long-note-no-loop-click-minus-24db-bed",
 "Binaural 'meet them where they are' staged rate ramp: two fixed-frequency sine voices hard-panned L/R, right voice automated by clip envelope from beta to alpha (and on to theta and back), fast early drop then slow glide, one long note per layer so there is no loop-restart click, sat at about -24 dB under a 60 BPM drone bed",
 "Build a binaural-beat layer as two fixed-frequency single-sine synth voices in one instrument rack (left voice fixed at the carrier, e.g. 200 Hz, panned hard left; right voice panned hard right), save per-state preset clips whose envelope sets the right voice to carrier+beat (204 = 4 Hz theta, 210 = 10 Hz alpha), and for a journey automate the right-voice frequency over time so the beat starts where a stressed listener is (beta, e.g. 15 Hz or 40->14 Hz), drops quickly in the first minute, then glides slowly into alpha (~8 Hz) and optionally theta and back to alpha, played as one very long MIDI note and mixed at about -24 dB under a 60 BPM drone bed.",
 ["A DAW with a synth whose oscillator can be set to a fixed frequency in Hz (e.g. Operator 'Fixed' mode) or any sine oscillator with Hz control","Clip or arrangement automation","Headphones for checking (binaural beats need separate L/R ears)"],
 [("carrier_hz","number","Fixed carrier for the left voice, e.g. 200 Hz",True),("journey","list","Beat-rate stages in Hz with durations, e.g. 15->8 over 5 min, or 40->14 with most of the drop in minute 1",True),("bed","audio","Drone/pad bed at 60 BPM (optional)",False)],
 [("binaural_layer","audio","Stereo L/R sine pair whose right voice follows the rate plan"),("preset_clips","midi","One clip per brainwave state (static) plus one ramp clip")],
 ["Use one oscillator per voice (other oscillators off); voices are identical except the right voice's frequency.",
  "Beat rate = right Hz - left Hz; keep the left voice fixed so the plan reads directly (e.g. 240->214 Hz means 40->14 Hz).",
  "Do not loop a short clip: a 1-bar loop re-triggers the note and clicks each bar. Draw one note across a very long clip (e.g. 144 bars at an 8-bar grid).",
  "Keep the layer in the background (about -24 dB in the mix); the beat works at low level, and loud sine tones are tiring.",
  "If mapping a controller to the beat, limit its range to carrier..carrier+50 Hz (beta/gamma ceiling) so a knob turn moves gently.",
  "The ramp moves the right voice's frequency smoothly over minutes, which is a slow planned glide, not random pitch wobble.",
  "Headphones only; on speakers use the library's mono/isochronic variants instead.",NOCLAIM,ABL],
 [("Create a MIDI track with a fixed-frequency synth (Operator: disable oscillators B-D, set A to Fixed, 200 Hz). Group it into an instrument rack and duplicate the chain; name the chains Left and Right and pan them hard left and hard right.","Synth + instrument rack","Two identical 200 Hz sines, one per ear"),
  ("Draw one note in a long clip (e.g. 144 bars, grid set to 8 bars, note stretched across the whole clip). The pitch of the MIDI note does not matter because the frequency is fixed.","MIDI clip","Continuous tone with no restart click"),
  ("Make static preset clips: in each clip's envelope set the Right chain's frequency to carrier + beat (204 Hz = 4 Hz theta, 210 Hz = 10 Hz alpha) and name the clip after the state.","Clip envelope","Library of one-click brainwave states"),
  ("Make a ramp clip: put a breakpoint at the start at the listener's likely state (e.g. 215 Hz = 15 Hz low beta) and one at about 4-5 minutes at alpha (207-208 Hz). Name it '15 Hz beta to 8 Hz alpha'.","Clip envelope","Beat glides from 15 to 8 Hz over ~5 min"),
  ("Longer journey (optional): start at 240 Hz (40 Hz) and drop most of the way within the first minute (e.g. to ~225 Hz by bar 17 at 60 BPM), then continue slowly to 214 Hz (14 Hz). Chain further stages into alpha, theta, then back to alpha. Copy the clips to the arrangement timeline for a fixed release, or launch them live from clip view for sessions.","Arrangement automation","Staged descent: fast exit from high beta, slow settle"),
  ("Set the project to 60 BPM, add a drone or pad bed (plus optional nature/water ambience and a soft lead), and pull the binaural layer down to about -24 dB. Check on headphones.","Mixer","Beat felt under the music rather than heard on top")],
 ["Left channel stays at the carrier; right channel frequency matches the plan at each breakpoint (beat = R - L).","No click or level dip at bar boundaries (single long note).","Binaural layer sits about 20+ dB below the bed's peaks.","Rate curve drops faster at the start than at the end when the staged plan is used."],
 ["binaural","brainwave","rate-ramp","entrainment","meditation","fixed-frequency","clip-envelope","drone-bed","60-bpm"])

# 2 ---------------------------------------------- seamless transition between themes (GXNWpGSkgBo)
S("GXNWpGSkgBo","How to Compose a Seamless Transition Between Themes",
 "theme-to-theme-transition-list-differences-sentence-form-half-cadence-iv-start-borrowed-motifs-register-walk-hybrid-timbre-breath",
 "Seamless transition between two contrasting themes: list every difference (timbre, register, key, dynamics, tempo, meter, texture, melodic range), write an 8-bar sentence that starts on IV and ends on a half cadence in the new key, carry the old theme's last figure in and foreshadow the new theme's motif, walk the register down, share the line across both timbres, step the dynamics through the midpoint and leave a breath",
 "Write a transition section that morphs one theme into a very different one: (1) list the differences between the themes (timbre, pitch register, key, dynamics, tempo, meter, texture, melodic range/motif), (2) structure the transition as a forward-moving sentence that ends on a half cadence so it stays unresolved until the new theme, (3) start on a less stable chord (IV of the old key) and modulate (relative keys need little), (4) begin the melody with the old theme's last figure and fold in the new theme's signature figure as foreshadowing, (5) walk the melody's register toward the new theme's register over 8 bars, (6) give the line to a hybrid of both timbres, (7) set dynamics between the two themes and decrescendo into a short breath before the new theme.",
 ["A DAW or notation with MIDI; two contrasting themes (or sections) already written","Synth or orchestral patches for both themes' timbres"],
 [("theme_a","midi","Outgoing theme (e.g. high, quiet, homophonic, major)",True),("theme_b","midi","Incoming theme (e.g. low, loud, monophonic, minor)",True)],
 [("transition","midi","8-bar sentence transition ending on a half cadence in theme B's key"),("difference_table","notes","The difference list and how each one is bridged or left alone")],
 ["A sentence (short idea, repeat, then a driving continuation) moves forward; a balanced period does not. Use a sentence for transitions.",
  "End on a half cadence (V of the new key) so the incompleteness resolves only when theme B arrives.",
  "Not every difference needs bridging: small tempo (e.g. 109->120 BPM) or meter (3/4->4/4) changes can pass unnoticed after a breath.",
  "A brief decrescendo/breath before a loud new theme still reads as a rising dynamic plan at the larger scale (mp -> mf -> ff).",
  "Melody stays steady and composed; morph by register and orchestration, not pitch bends.",ABL],
 [("List the differences between theme A and theme B in a table: timbre, register, key, dynamics, tempo, meter, texture, melodic range and signature figure.","Notes","Bridge plan per difference"),
  ("Plan an 8-bar sentence (2-bar idea, 2-bar repeat/variation, 4-bar continuation) that ends on a half cadence in theme B's key.","Form","Forward-driving, unresolved ending"),
  ("Harmony: start on IV of theme A's key (e.g. Bb in F major) rather than I for instant motion, then take a conventional path to V of theme B's key (relative minor: D minor -> half cadence on A).","Chord track","Key change that sounds inevitable"),
  ("Melody: open with theme A's last figure (e.g. its suspension F->E), add theme B's signature figure (e.g. a 16th-note half-step neighbour) as foreshadowing, and step the line toward theme B's register across the 8 bars; drop the whole transition an octave if a large gap remains at the join.","Lead MIDI","Both themes audible in the bridge; register gap closed"),
  ("Timbre: give the melody to a blend of both sound families (e.g. violas, cellos and horns between strings and brass; in synths, layer A's pad voice with B's brass/saw voice). Fill harmony above with a quiet arpeggio that also uses theme B's neighbour figure, and thin it out near the end toward B's texture.","Orchestration/layers","Hybrid colour; texture narrows toward B"),
  ("Dynamics: set the transition between the two themes (mp -> mf -> ff), then decrescendo in the last bar and leave a short breath (rest) before theme B.","Volume/velocity automation","Theme B lands with impact but not as a non-sequitur")],
 ["Transition ends on V of theme B's key (half cadence).","The first melodic figure of the transition matches the last figure of theme A; theme B's signature figure appears at least once before B.","Melody register at the end of the transition is within an octave of theme B's first note.","Dynamic level of the transition lies between the two themes, with a breath at the join."],
 ["transition","arrangement","form","sentence","half-cadence","modulation","foreshadowing","orchestration","cinematic","theme-change"])

# 3 ---------------------------------------------- tension via end-of-bar dissonance + silence (IuscQShlL58)
S("IuscQShlL58","Tips and Tricks for Creating Tension in Electronic Music",
 "end-of-bar-dissonant-pad-minor-sixth-over-root-resolve-on-downbeat-early-resolve-for-energy-drop-silence-break-freeze-reverb-size-riser",
 "Electronic tension levers: a pad on the minor sixth (or fourth) over the bass root only at the end of the bar resolving to a consonant note on the downbeat, an early resolve before the bar when energy should drop into a breakdown, a short silence before the kick and bass return, and a freeze-reverb riser whose size is automated from consonant into dissonant territory",
 "Create tension and release in an electronic arrangement with dissonance placement rather than new material: over a static kick+bass on root C, bring in a pad on a dissonant interval (G# = minor sixth, or F = fourth, versus the consonant G fifth) only at the end of a bar and resolve it on the next downbeat; resolve before the downbeat instead when the next section drops energy (breakdown); cut to a few beats of silence before the kick and bass return; and render a freeze-reverb riser that starts on a consonant chord and is automated (reverb size) into dissonance, with dry/wet set higher than usual and faded in.",
 ["A DAW with MIDI and automation; a kick + bass loop on a root note","A pad synth with filter cutoff automation","A reverb with a freeze/infinite mode and a size parameter (or any long frozen reverb)"],
 [("root","string","Bass/kick root note, e.g. C",True),("section_plan","notes","Which bar ends get tension, which section drops energy",True)],
 [("tension_pad","midi","End-of-bar dissonant pad notes with downbeat resolution"),("silence_breaks","notes","Bar positions of 1-2 beat full-mix silences"),("freeze_riser","audio","Rendered freeze-reverb riser")],
 ["Interval rule from the source: the perfect fifth (C-G) fits the root's harmonics and sounds settled; the minor sixth (C-G#) or the fourth (C-F) sounds tense over a C root.",
  "Tension sits at the end of the bar and resolves on the next downbeat; resolving before the downbeat signals an energy drop.",
  "Silence works because full-spectrum dance music rarely stops: break for a few beats, then the regular kick+bass snaps back as resolution.",
  "The source also suggests a glide up into the tense note and random note generation; this skill uses stepped notes only (no glide, no random notes) to keep pitch steady (Matt's preference). The freeze-size move is a single, slow automated curve.",
  "Frequency shifters and unison detune are named as other dissonance sources; use sparingly and keep them out of the lead melody.",ABL,EARLY],
 [("Loop a kick and bass on the root (e.g. C). Write a pad that holds the root for most of the bar, then steps to the minor sixth (G#) or fourth (F) for the last beat(s); bring its filter cutoff in by automation.","Pad MIDI + cutoff automation","Tense colour at each bar end"),
  ("Resolve on the downbeat: the next bar begins on a consonant note/chord (root or fifth). A/B against a version where the end-of-bar note is the fifth (G) to hear the difference.","Pad MIDI","Clear tension -> release each bar"),
  ("For an energy drop into a breakdown, resolve the pad just before the next downbeat (render the pad to audio for precise control of the reverb tail), then strip percussion and soften the synths in the new section.","Audio render + arrangement","Listener feels the energy fall"),
  ("Before a return, cut the full mix to silence for a few beats at the end of a build; the kick and bass re-enter on the downbeat.","Arrangement edit","Expectation gap, then strong resolution"),
  ("Freeze riser: play a consonant chord into a reverb with size and decay high, write automation, freeze immediately, let it hold, then automate size on a long curve so the frozen tone moves into dissonance. Raise dry/wet more than usual, render to audio and fade it in.","Freeze reverb + automation","Riser that starts in key and drifts tense"),
  ("Place renders and pad tension only at structural points (bar ends before section changes) so the levers keep their effect.","Arrangement","Tension used as punctuation, not wallpaper")],
 ["End-of-bar pad notes are a minor sixth or fourth above the bass root; downbeat notes are consonant (root/third/fifth).","At least one silence break of 1-2 beats precedes a full return.","Freeze riser is rendered audio with a fade-in and no click at its start."],
 ["tension","release","dissonance","intervals","silence","breakdown","freeze-reverb","riser","electronic"])

# 4 ---------------------------------------------- minimal dreamy ambient growth (POMnaOf92UI)
S("POMnaOf92UI","How to Make Dark, Dreamy Ambient from Scratch in Ableton Live 12",
 "four-part-dreamy-ambient-common-tone-imaj7-iii-loop-melody-grows-by-unmuting-notes-pad-filter-opens-grain-delay-shimmer-air-ramp",
 "Four-part dreamy ambient that grows without new tracks: an 8-bar Imaj7 -> iii common-tone pad loop with a very slow shared LFO, a filtered looped vocal pad, a dark detuned saw bass, and a syncopated pluck melody that starts with most notes muted and gains notes toward the end, while the pad filter opens and an automated grain-delay shimmer adds air across the track",
 "Make a dark, dreamy ambient piece (~94 BPM, E major) from four parts and grow it by density and brightness rather than by adding tracks: (1) a swarm/detuned two-oscillator pad on an 8-bar Imaj7 -> iii loop (the chords share three notes; revoice with octave moves), slow attack, full sustain, long release, one very slow low-depth LFO on timbre and spacing, chorus and a huge reverb; (2) a sampled vocal pad on loop, softened with bit-reduction + low-pass and big reverb; (3) a mono bass from two slightly detuned saws an octave down at full sustain with a slow notch-filter LFO and low cut-off; (4) a sine-to-triangle wavetable pluck melody with syncopated off-beat eighths, light saturation and compression, dotted-eighth ping-pong echo and reverb, then arrange it by muting most melody notes early and restoring them (plus a few more) toward the end, duplicating it with more echo, ducking everything lightly to the lead, opening the pad filter across the whole track and automating a grain delay in for shimmering top end.",
 ["A DAW with a two-oscillator or wavetable synth, sampler with loop mode, chorus, large reverb, echo/ping-pong delay, grain delay (or any pitch-shifted delay), auto filter, LFO mapping"],
 [("key","string","Major key, e.g. E major",True),("tempo","number","About 94 BPM",True),("vocal_sample","audio","Short sustained vocal phrase to loop (optional)",False)],
 [("track","audio","Four-part ambient track whose melody density and top-end air increase toward the end"),("arrangement_notes","notes","Melody mute map, filter and grain-delay automation curves")],
 ["Chord rule from the source: in ambient, move between chords that share most notes (E major 7 -> G# minor shares G#, B, D#) so changes are smooth, not tense.",
  "Pad LFO: very slow (over 2+ bars, then slower) and low depth so movement is barely noticeable.",
  "The source randomises the vocal loop length and adds bass glide; both are left out here to keep pitch and loops steady (Matt's preference). Keep any melody/pitch modulation free of random variation.",
  "Echo modulation stays subtle (rate ~1, low amount) so repeats do not wobble in pitch.",
  "Growth comes from un-muting melody notes and opening filters/air, not from adding many tracks.",ABL,EARLY],
 [("Set ~94 BPM. On a two-oscillator synth write an 8-bar loop of Imaj7 -> iii (E major 7 -> G# minor), revoicing with octave moves for smooth voice leading and doubling bass notes to fatten. Link both oscillator amp envelopes: slow attack, full sustain, longer release.","Pad synth + MIDI","Smooth, tension-free pad loop"),
  ("Map one very slow, low-depth LFO to the pad's timbre/motion and oscillator spacing; raise spread for width; tune the second oscillator a little brighter with its filter lowered; add chorus (a bit of feedback) and a very large reverb.","LFO + chorus + reverb","Barely moving, wide, dreamy pad"),
  ("Load a vocal phrase into a sampler in loop-sustain mode; soften it with bit-reduction (rate down) followed by a smooth low-pass, then the large reverb. Keep its loop length fixed.","Sampler + bit-reduction + filter","Organic vocal texture under the pad"),
  ("Bass: wavetable with two slightly detuned saws an octave down, full-sustain envelope, mono; slow LFO into a notch auto-filter for gentle movement, a second filter with cut-off pulled back, light chorus and EQ.","Bass synth + auto filter","Dark, slowly moving low layer"),
  ("Lead: wavetable pluck between sine and triangle with unison and a slow free-running (non-retriggered) LFO on wavetable position; write a syncopated melody on off-beat eighths; add saturation, compression to soften the attack, dotted-eighth ping-pong echo with very slight modulation, and reverb.","Lead synth + echo","Moving but steady-pitched melody"),
  ("Arrange by density: early on, mute most melody notes; restore them gradually and add a few extra notes so the line is fullest near the end. Duplicate the melody track with the echo turned up for a wash. Add a rain/ambience bed and an ambient guitar with highs boosted on the sides. Duck all parts lightly to the lead.","Arrangement","Same four parts, rising activity"),
  ("Automate the pad's filter to open across the whole track, and automate a grain delay (pitched up, low mix) in from about the midpoint so shimmering top-end air grows toward the end.","Filter + grain delay automation","Track gets brighter and airier as it grows")],
 ["Pad chords share at least two notes between neighbours.","Melody note count in the last section is greater than in the first.","Spectral centroid / high-band energy rises from start to end (pad filter + grain delay).","No randomised loop length or pitch glide in the render."],
 ["ambient","arrangement","density-growth","pad","common-tone","melody-muting","air","grain-delay","shimmer","dreamy"])
